mainmenu "coex154 - the P3.5 802.15.4 blackout measurement"

choice
	prompt "Role"
	default COEX154_ROLE_RX
	help
	  Which end of the measurement this image is. Both ends are the same
	  application on purpose: the frame layout and the sequence arithmetic
	  have to agree exactly, and two programs that agree by inspection are
	  two programs that stop agreeing.

config COEX154_ROLE_RX
	bool "Receiver - counts frames and reports exact loss"
	help
	  The DUT. Sits in promiscuous receive and tallies frames by the
	  sequence number in their payload, so loss is computed from what was
	  SENT rather than from what was expected to be sent. This is the end
	  that also runs radiant_core, and therefore the end whose radio is
	  being taken away.

config COEX154_ROLE_TX
	bool "Transmitter - the traffic generator"
	help
	  The reference end. Sends a counted, evenly-spaced stream and nothing
	  else. It must NOT run radiant_core: a generator whose own radio is
	  arbitrated cannot tell "the DUT did not hear it" from "it was never
	  sent", which is the one distinction the whole measurement rests on.

endchoice

config COEX154_CHANNEL
	int "802.15.4 channel"
	range 11 26
	default 26
	help
	  26 by default, and deliberately the top of the band: it is the
	  furthest 802.15.4 channel from the 2.4 GHz BLE advertising channels
	  (2402/2426/2480 MHz) and from most Wi-Fi. This measurement is about
	  the ARBITER taking the radio away, so ambient air must not be a
	  competing explanation for a missing frame.

config COEX154_PAN_ID
	hex "PAN id"
	default 0xca7e

config COEX154_PSDU_LEN
	int "PSDU length in bytes, including the 2-byte FCS"
	range 21 127
	default 127
	help
	  127 is the maximum, ~4.25 ms on air at 250 kbps, and it is the row
	  docs/radiant-bridge.md section 7.3a predicts 18 % (24 % with the
	  follow-on reserve) for. Set 40 for the other column of that table.

	  The floor of 21 is the header (9) plus the payload this application
	  needs to identify a frame as its own and number it (10) plus FCS (2).

config COEX154_TX_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Milliseconds between transmitted frames"
	range 5 10000
	default 20
	help
	  20 ms is 50 frames a second, which gathers a statistically useful
	  count in a minute without saturating the channel - at 4.25 ms a frame
	  that is a 21 % duty cycle, well clear of the CCA backoff region where
	  the generator's own retries would become the thing being measured.

config COEX154_TX_COUNT
	int "How many frames to send, 0 for endless"
	default 0

config COEX154_ANT_MASTERS
	int "ANT+ master channels the receiver runs alongside 802.15.4"
	depends on COEX154_ROLE_RX && RADIANT_CORE
	range 0 8
	default 8
	help
	  THE BLACKOUT GENERATOR, and it is masters rather than a receiver on
	  purpose.

	  docs/radiant-bridge.md section 7.3a models loss as N blackouts per
	  period against frame duration, and its rows are labelled "tracked
	  sensors". A RECEIVER cannot produce that: a channel that is searching
	  blacks out on a sweep schedule, and a channel that is tracking blacks
	  out only for as long as a real sensor keeps transmitting to it. Both
	  make the ANT duty cycle a property of the room. A MASTER transmits
	  every channel period whether anyone is listening or not, so N is
	  exactly this number and the measurement is repeatable on a bench with
	  no sensors on it at all.

	  8 is section 7.3a's headline row - "8 tracked sensors, ~4 % aggregate
	  duty, 18 % of max-size frames clipped" - which is the figure P3.5
	  exists to confirm or refute. 16 and 32 are the other rows and this
	  hardware cannot reach them; 8 channels is the ceiling the range above
	  records rather than hides.

	  0 turns ANT off while leaving radiant_core linked and initialised,
	  which is a THIRD arm of the A/B and a useful one: it separates the
	  cost of the arbiter merely being present from the cost of the air it
	  takes away.

config COEX154_ARBITRATED
	bool
	default y if RADIANT_CORE_BACKEND_NRF_GATE_MPSL
	select NRF_802154_MULTIPROTOCOL_SUPPORT
	help
	  Not user-facing. Exists solely to reach
	  NRF_802154_MULTIPROTOCOL_SUPPORT, which has no prompt and so cannot be
	  set from a .conf file at all - assigning it there is a hard Kconfig
	  error, not a silent drop, which is the one merciful thing about it.

	  THIS SELECT IS REDUNDANT, AND THE PARAGRAPH THAT USED TO BE HERE WAS
	  WRONG. It claimed the symbol is "selected only by the OpenThread
	  integration", so that an application reaching nrf_802154 directly would
	  get a driver that ignores MPSL and programs the RADIO over the top of
	  the gate's timeslot. Checked against the generated .config on
	  2026-08-12: not so. NRF_802154_RADIO_DRIVER=y is sufficient on its own -
	  nrfxlib's Kconfig.nrfxlib defaults NRF_802154_SOURCE_NRFXLIB and then
	  NRF_802154_SL, NRF_802154_SL does `select MPSL`, and MPSL does
	  `select NRF_802154_MULTIPROTOCOL_SUPPORT if NRF_802154_RADIO_DRIVER`.
	  Every coex154 image already had both symbols set.

	  Kept anyway, and only because the intent is worth stating: nothing in
	  this application would fail if it were deleted, and a reader who deletes
	  it should know they are relying on an nrfxlib choice default rather than
	  on anything here.

	  THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE A/B IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. This symbol cannot
	  key arbitration off the control arm, because nothing can: on nRF54L
	  there is no build of this driver that does not talk to MPSL. So the
	  "uncontended" arm is 15.4-with-an-arbiter-and-no-competitor, not
	  15.4-with-no-arbiter, and the A/B measures what a second stack's DEMAND
	  costs rather than what arbitration itself costs. Getting the latter
	  would mean NRF_802154_SL_OPENSOURCE=y or NRF_802154_SOURCE_HAL_NORDIC=y,
	  which swaps the Service Layer wholesale - a different driver, not this
	  one with arbitration removed - and is therefore not a control arm for
	  this experiment either.

config COEX154_REPORT_S
	int "Seconds between receiver reports"
	default 10

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
